1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:01,935
Spell the word SCHOOL.

2
00:00:01,935 --> 00:00:02,841
Six letters.

3
00:00:02,841 --> 00:00:04,672
S, C, H, O, O, L.

4
00:00:05,072 --> 00:00:07,340
Now write the set of its letters.

5
00:00:07,340 --> 00:00:08,240
Only five.

6
00:00:08,190 --> 00:00:09,803
S, C, H, O, L.

7
00:00:10,253 --> 00:00:13,409
Aarav spotted this while
copying his notes last night.

8
00:00:13,758 --> 00:00:16,651
Six letters went in,
but only five came out.

9
00:00:16,651 --> 00:00:18,439
Where did the second O go?

10
00:00:18,939 --> 00:00:22,483
The answer hides inside the
two ways of writing a set.

11
00:00:22,483 --> 00:00:24,848
Roster form, and set builder form.

12
00:00:25,498 --> 00:00:26,563
Quick recall.

13
00:00:26,563 --> 00:00:31,449
Roster form lists every element of a
set inside curly brackets, with commas

14
00:00:31,449 --> 00:00:32,514
between them.

15
00:00:32,864 --> 00:00:35,302
But a roster list follows two rules.

16
00:00:35,302 --> 00:00:37,938
Rule one: no element is written twice.

17
00:00:38,338 --> 00:00:41,459
Watch the letters of
SCHOOL drop into the set.

18
00:00:41,459 --> 00:00:43,450
S is new, so it goes in.

19
00:00:43,450 --> 00:00:44,350
C is new.

20
00:00:44,339 --> 00:00:45,239
H is new.

21
00:00:45,228 --> 00:00:46,128
O is new.

22
00:00:46,517 --> 00:00:47,878
Now the second O.

23
00:00:47,878 --> 00:00:51,342
A set only asks one
question: is O in, or out?

24
00:00:51,342 --> 00:00:53,953
O is already in, so nothing changes.

25
00:00:54,353 --> 00:00:55,282
L is new.

26
00:00:55,282 --> 00:00:58,016
So the set is S, C, H, O, L.

27
00:00:58,016 --> 00:00:59,145
Five elements.

28
00:00:59,145 --> 00:01:01,256
Writing O twice adds nothing.

29
00:01:01,906 --> 00:01:03,822
Try it with the word BETTER.

30
00:01:03,822 --> 00:01:07,511
Six letters again, but the
set is just B, E, T, R.

31
00:01:07,511 --> 00:01:08,531
Four elements.

32
00:01:09,181 --> 00:01:11,575
Rule two: order does not matter.

33
00:01:11,575 --> 00:01:12,694
Side by side.

34
00:01:13,094 --> 00:01:14,997
A word cares about order.

35
00:01:14,997 --> 00:01:16,795
T, E, A spells TEA.

36
00:01:16,795 --> 00:01:18,593
A, T, E spells ATE.

37
00:01:18,593 --> 00:01:21,078
Same letters, two different words.

38
00:01:21,478 --> 00:01:22,885
A set does not care.

39
00:01:22,885 --> 00:01:26,697
The set T, E, A and the set
A, T, E are the same set.

40
00:01:26,697 --> 00:01:28,422
Only what is inside counts.

41
00:01:29,022 --> 00:01:29,998
Pause and think.

42
00:01:29,998 --> 00:01:33,466
Are one, two, two, three and
three, two, one the same set?

43
00:01:36,666 --> 00:01:37,566
Yes!

44
00:01:37,181 --> 00:01:39,726
Drop the repeat and ignore the order.

45
00:01:39,726 --> 00:01:42,483
Both sets are just one, two and three.

46
00:01:43,133 --> 00:01:44,501
Now the second form.

47
00:01:44,501 --> 00:01:47,717
Set builder form writes the
rule instead of the list.

48
00:01:48,067 --> 00:01:49,105
Here is one.

49
00:01:49,105 --> 00:01:54,117
The set of all x, such that x is a
positive integer, and x squared is less

50
00:01:54,117 --> 00:01:55,045
than forty.

51
00:01:55,445 --> 00:01:59,391
To turn it into roster form,
we test x one value at a time.

52
00:01:59,791 --> 00:02:00,691
x is one.

53
00:02:00,660 --> 00:02:03,760
One squared is one,
which is less than forty.

54
00:02:03,760 --> 00:02:04,886
So one is in.

55
00:02:05,286 --> 00:02:06,473
Two gives four.

56
00:02:06,473 --> 00:02:07,761
Three gives nine.

57
00:02:07,761 --> 00:02:09,150
Four gives sixteen.

58
00:02:09,150 --> 00:02:10,800
Five gives twenty five.

59
00:02:10,800 --> 00:02:12,350
Six gives thirty six.

60
00:02:12,350 --> 00:02:13,859
All of them are in.

61
00:02:14,259 --> 00:02:15,870
Seven gives forty nine.

62
00:02:15,870 --> 00:02:18,395
That crosses forty, so seven is out.

63
00:02:18,395 --> 00:02:22,344
Every bigger x gives an even
bigger square, so we can stop.

64
00:02:22,994 --> 00:02:26,609
The roster form is one,
two, three, four, five, six.

65
00:02:26,609 --> 00:02:28,924
One short rule became a list of six.

66
00:02:29,574 --> 00:02:31,271
Going backwards is harder.

67
00:02:31,271 --> 00:02:32,447
Here is a list.

68
00:02:32,447 --> 00:02:35,992
One, four, nine, sixteen,
twenty five, and so on.

69
00:02:36,392 --> 00:02:37,292
Step one.

70
00:02:37,246 --> 00:02:39,660
Ask what each element is made of.

71
00:02:39,660 --> 00:02:41,326
One is one times one.

72
00:02:41,326 --> 00:02:43,043
Four is two times two.

73
00:02:43,043 --> 00:02:44,969
Nine is three times three.

74
00:02:45,719 --> 00:02:46,619
Step two.

75
00:02:46,565 --> 00:02:47,887
Spot the pattern.

76
00:02:47,887 --> 00:02:51,067
Every element is a natural
number times itself.

77
00:02:51,067 --> 00:02:52,771
We call that number n.

78
00:02:53,521 --> 00:02:54,421
Step three.

79
00:02:54,420 --> 00:02:55,573
Write the rule.

80
00:02:55,573 --> 00:02:58,846
x equals n squared, where
n is a natural number.

81
00:02:59,596 --> 00:03:04,692
So the set is all x, such that x
equals n squared, and n belongs to N.

82
00:03:04,692 --> 00:03:07,275
The three dots became one short rule.

83
00:03:07,925 --> 00:03:09,638
Notice the two letters here.

84
00:03:09,638 --> 00:03:11,456
x is the element of the set.

85
00:03:11,456 --> 00:03:13,634
n is only a counter that builds it.

86
00:03:14,034 --> 00:03:16,432
n runs one, two, three, and so on.

87
00:03:16,432 --> 00:03:18,961
x is what comes out: one, four, nine.

88
00:03:18,961 --> 00:03:20,551
Only x lives in the set.

89
00:03:21,151 --> 00:03:22,241
Pause and think.

90
00:03:22,241 --> 00:03:23,659
Is eight in this set?

91
00:03:26,859 --> 00:03:27,759
No!

92
00:03:27,285 --> 00:03:30,148
Two squared is four and
three squared is nine.

93
00:03:30,148 --> 00:03:34,049
No natural number squared gives
eight, so eight does not belong.

94
00:03:34,699 --> 00:03:35,770
Remember this.

95
00:03:35,770 --> 00:03:39,163
Roster form lists each
element once, in any order.

96
00:03:39,163 --> 00:03:42,616
Set builder form gives the
one rule they all follow.

97
00:03:43,316 --> 00:03:45,051
Now, one worked example.

98
00:03:45,401 --> 00:03:47,673
Write this set in set builder form.

99
00:03:47,673 --> 00:03:52,207
One half, two thirds, three quarters,
and so on, up to six sevenths.

100
00:03:52,707 --> 00:03:53,607
Step one.

101
00:03:53,476 --> 00:03:56,372
The tops run one, two, three, up to six.

102
00:03:56,372 --> 00:03:57,534
Call the top n.

103
00:03:58,284 --> 00:03:59,184
Step two.

104
00:03:59,107 --> 00:04:01,746
Every bottom is one more than its top.

105
00:04:01,746 --> 00:04:03,823
So the bottom is n plus one.

106
00:04:04,573 --> 00:04:05,473
Step three.

107
00:04:05,440 --> 00:04:08,118
n is a natural number, from one to six.

108
00:04:08,868 --> 00:04:14,124
So the set is all x, such that x
equals n over n plus one, where n is a

109
00:04:14,124 --> 00:04:16,422
natural number from one to six.

110
00:04:17,072 --> 00:04:18,537
Now, the exam trap.

111
00:04:18,887 --> 00:04:22,722
How many elements are in the set
of letters of the word COMMITTEE?

112
00:04:22,722 --> 00:04:24,263
Many students count nine.

113
00:04:24,663 --> 00:04:27,025
But M, T and E each appear twice.

114
00:04:27,025 --> 00:04:30,359
Write each letter once: C, O, M, I, T, E.

115
00:04:30,359 --> 00:04:32,368
The set has only six elements.

116
00:04:33,018 --> 00:04:34,590
Where is this asked?

117
00:04:34,590 --> 00:04:39,388
In PUC board exams, as write this
set in roster form or in set builder

118
00:04:39,388 --> 00:04:40,288
form.

119
00:04:39,978 --> 00:04:44,112
And in JEE Main, as how many
elements does this set have.

120
00:04:44,712 --> 00:04:45,899
Exam level now.

121
00:04:45,899 --> 00:04:48,435
How many elements does this set have?

122
00:04:48,435 --> 00:04:49,672
Pause and solve.

123
00:04:55,072 --> 00:04:56,240
The answer is B.

124
00:04:56,240 --> 00:04:57,140
Eight.

125
00:04:57,292 --> 00:04:59,060
First, rewrite the rule.

126
00:04:59,060 --> 00:05:03,841
n squared minus six n plus ten equals
n minus three, squared, plus one.

127
00:05:04,341 --> 00:05:08,764
So n equals two and n equals
four give the same value, two.

128
00:05:08,764 --> 00:05:12,460
And n equals one and n
equals five both give five.

129
00:05:12,960 --> 00:05:15,708
Ten values of n, but two values repeat.

130
00:05:15,708 --> 00:05:18,643
Ten minus two leaves
eight different elements.

131
00:05:19,243 --> 00:05:20,702
Here is your recap.

132
00:05:20,702 --> 00:05:22,201
Screenshot this card.

133
00:05:25,401 --> 00:05:28,400
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

134
00:05:28,800 --> 00:05:29,700
One.

135
00:05:29,279 --> 00:05:32,916
Write the set of letters of
the word APPLE in roster form.

136
00:05:33,816 --> 00:05:34,716
Two.

137
00:05:34,303 --> 00:05:39,107
Write the set two, four, eight,
sixteen, thirty two in set builder form.

138
00:05:40,007 --> 00:05:40,907
Three.

139
00:05:40,609 --> 00:05:45,205
In the set of all x such that x equals
n squared, which letter lives in

140
00:05:45,205 --> 00:05:46,105
the set?

141
00:05:47,064 --> 00:05:50,143
Next time: the set of even
primes bigger than two.

142
00:05:50,143 --> 00:05:52,348
Is it nothing, or is it something?

143
00:05:52,348 --> 00:05:55,293
We meet the empty set,
and sets that never end.
